---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RelatedGuides from '../../components/RelatedGuides.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';

const path = '/guides/what-is-an-hwid-ban';
const title = 'What Is an HWID Ban?';
const description =
  'A plain-language definition of a hardware ID ban: what it blocks, how it shows up in game, and how it differs from a banned account.';
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides' },
  { name: title, path },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} breadcrumbs={crumbs} article>
  <article class="shell py-12 sm:py-16" data-reveal>
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <header class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-5xl sm:text-6xl">{title}</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-xl text-muted">
        An HWID ban follows the computer. A new account on that same PC can still be stopped, because
        the penalty is stored against the machine.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">Updated {site.updatedLabel}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body mt-10">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        HWID means hardware ID. A hardware ID ban, sometimes called a device ban, is a penalty
        stored against a fingerprint of the machine rather than only against a username. The game
        can refuse the computer even when the login has never been punished before.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why a new login on the same PC often changes nothing. An HWID ban is stored against
        the machine, so the computer is still refused.
      </p>

      <h2>Hardware ID, in ordinary language</h2>
      <p>
        A hardware ID is not a single serial number printed on the case. Anti-cheat software
        collects several identifiers from the PC and derives a value, or a set of values, that is
        stable enough to recognize the machine later. Disks, motherboards, network adapters, and
        firmware data are the kinds of inputs people discuss. Publishers do not document the exact
        mix, and they change it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You do not need the formula to use the idea. If the publisher can recognize the computer,
        it can attach a ban to that recognition. The account is then only one of the things being
        checked at login.
      </p>

      <h2>What players notice</h2>
      <p>The reports that actually match an HWID ban tend to look like this:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The original account is banned, permanently or for a long term.</li>
        <li>A new account on the same PC is kicked, refused, or banned very quickly.</li>
        <li>Changing networks, or using a VPN, does not alter the result.</li>
        <li>The same new account may be able to play on a different computer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The last point matters. A hardware ban is about the machine that was recorded, not about
        every device you own. A laptop that never played the game is not automatically included.
        Consoles are a separate enforcement path. A PC spoofer does not change a PlayStation or
        Xbox restriction.
      </p>

      <h2>Account bans that get mistaken for HWID bans</h2>
      <p>
        Plenty of messages say “banned” and mean the account. Phone verification, a linked email, a
        payment method, or a shared household PC can also make a new account fail for reasons that
        have nothing to do with a hardware ID. Family sharing and a second account created inside
        the same launcher sometimes inherit a penalty without a device ban existing at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before you treat the problem as an HWID ban, read the notice for an end date, try to see
        whether it names the account, and check whether another trusted account on that PC can
        still play. The <a href="/games">game notes</a> point out titles where hardware bans are
        common, and titles where they are not.
      </p>

      <h2>What an HWID spoofer is</h2>
      <p>
        An HWID spoofer is software that presents different hardware identifiers from the ones
        stored with the ban. People use it when the restriction follows the PC and a new account
        alone is not enough. It does not appeal the ban, it does not log into the banned account,
        and it does not move rank, items, or purchases.
      </p>
      <p>
        The spoofer offered here is aimed at hardware bans in the games on that list, and it is
        offered as undetected. Undetected means it is not currently flagged on its own by the
        anti-cheat those games use. Anti-cheat updates can change that. It is a status, not a
        warranty that a specific ban will clear.
      </p>

      <h2>Before you pay</h2>
      <p>
        Buy a spoofer for a hardware ID ban. If you want the general model first, read
        <a href="/guides/how-hwid-bans-work">how HWID bans work</a>. Most games also forbid
        ban evasion in their terms. Breaking those terms can bring further penalties from the
        publisher.
      </p>
      <RelatedGuides current={path} />
    </div>
  </article>
  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>
